Yes — that task can legitimately take several minutes because your Helm command includes:

```yaml
--wait
--timeout 10m
```

So Ansible will sit on:

```text
TASK [cilium : Install or upgrade Cilium from Artifactory]
```

until Helm sees the Cilium resources become ready, or the 10-minute timeout is reached.

While it is running, open another terminal and check what is happening on `master01`.

First, check whether Helm created the release:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a 'KUBECONFIG=/etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml /usr/bin/helm list -A'
```

Then inspect Cilium pods:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get pods -n kube-system -o wide | grep -i cilium'
```

Also check all cluster pods:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get pods -A -o wide'
```

And nodes:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get nodes -o wide'
```

If Cilium pods exist but are not becoming `Running`, inspect them:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=cilium'
```

Then describe one failing pod:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml describe pod -n kube-system $(/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=cilium -o jsonpath="{.items[0].metadata.name}")'
```

Also check recent events because image-pull or CNI errors will show up there:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a '/var/lib/rancher/rke2/bin/kubectl --kubeconfig /etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail -50'
```

The important distinction is:

```text
No Cilium pods at all
→ Helm may still be pulling/rendering/chart processing

Cilium pods Pending/ImagePullBackOff
→ registry / image / scheduling issue

Cilium pods CrashLoopBackOff
→ Cilium config problem

Cilium pods Running but Helm still waiting
→ another resource such as cilium-operator is not Ready
```

Because your cluster previously had all three nodes `NotReady` due to no CNI, it is normal for this first Cilium install to take longer than later upgrades.

If you want to see exactly what Helm itself is waiting on, run:

```bash
ansible master01 \
  -i inventory/dev/hosts.ini \
  -b \
  -m shell \
  -a 'KUBECONFIG=/etc/rancher/rke2/rke2.yaml /usr/bin/helm status cilium -n kube-system'
```

That is the best command to run now while the Ansible task is still sitting there.



























